Shell.run's finish closure is @Sendable, as asyncAfter's block must be.

DispatchQueue.asyncAfter(deadline:execute:) takes a @Sendable block. Xcode 27.1's Swift
warns that passing the plain closure "may introduce data races", and the management tests
build with warnings as errors, so they failed on GitHub's runner (Xcode 27.2 is silent).
Everything finish captures is already Sendable (Once and OutputCollector are @unchecked
Sendable, the continuation is), so declaring it @Sendable changes nothing at run time.
Upstream candidate: brand-free, the same warning on Tolkara's own build.

--- a/management/App/Core/Shell.swift
+++ b/management/App/Core/Shell.swift
@@ -36,7 +36,7 @@
                 // Done when the process has exited and its output has ended; a
                 // grandchild may keep the pipe open, so do not wait long for that.
                 let once = Once()
-                let finish = {
+                let finish: @Sendable () -> Void = {
                     once.run {
                         pipe.fileHandleForReading.readabilityHandler = nil
                         continuation.resume(returning: CommandResult(status: process.terminationStatus, output: collector.finish()))
